t           ['require (primitive-entry\n   410\t                       'require\n   411\t                       (fn [spec]\n   412\t                         (let [ns-sym (if (vector? spec) (first spec) spec)]\n   413\t                           (effect/make :module/require {:module ns-sym})))\n   414\t                       :effectful [1] #{:module/require})]\n   415\t           ['try-require (primitive-entry\n   416\t                           'try-require\n   417\t                           (fn [spec]\n   418\t                             (let [ns-sym (if (vector? spec) (first spec) spec)]\n   419\t                               (effect/make :module/try-require\n   420\t                                            {:module ns-sym})))\n   421\t                           :effectful [1] #{:module/try-require})]])))\n   422\t\n   423\t\n   424\t(def ^:private primitive-profile-keys\n   425\t  [:yin.k/profile :yin.k/class :yin.k/arities :yin.k/effects\n   426\t   :yin.k/host-state])\n   427\t\n   428\t\n   429\t(def primitive-profiles\n   430\t  \"Portable projection of the standard registry, retained as a convenience.\"\n   895\t       :vm/current-continuation base\n   896\t       ;; fallback\n   897\t       (throw (ex-info \"Unknown AST node type in datoms\"\n   898\t                       {:type node-type, :root-id root-id}))))))\n   899\t\n   900\t\n   901\t(defn plain-data?\n   902\t  \"True when `x` is data a row or code datom may carry (§2.2, §2.5): scalars\n   903\t   and collections of them, never a host function or object. Jing's float64\n   904\t   carrier is a scalar: on JS it is how a float keeps its kind in a row.\n   905\t   Metadata travels with the value, so it must be plain data too.\"\n   906\t  [x]\n   907\t  (or (nil? x)\n   908\t      (and (cond (or (boolean? x) (number? x) (cbor/float64? x) (string? x)\n   909\t                     (keyword? x) (symbol? x))\n   910\t                 true\n   911\t                 (map? x) (and (every? plain-data? (keys x))\n   912\t                               (every? plain-data? (vals x)))\n   913\t                 (coll? x) (every? plain-data? x)\n   914\t                 :else false)\n   915\t           (plain-data? (meta x)))))\n   916\t\n   917\t\n   918\t(defn machine-data?\n   919\t  \"`plain-data?` for a value inside a machine payload: a closure or a\n   920\t   continuation, a host type only a kernel mints, counts as its payload,\n   921\t   as the plain map it was before D7. Rows and code datoms stay\n   922\t   `plain-data?`, plus the exact-integer big carriers (ruling 4).\"\n   923\t  [x]\n   924\t  (or (nil? x)\n   925\t      (if (values/host-typed? x)\n   926\t        (machine-data? (values/payload x))\n   927\t        (and (cond (or (boolean? x) (number? x)\n   928\t                       (integer-host/big-carrier? x) (cbor/float64? x)\n   929\t                       (string? x) (keyword? x) (symbol? x))\n   930\t                   true\n   931\t                   (map? x) (and (every? machine-data? (keys x))\n   932\t                                 (every? machine-data? (vals x)))\n   933\t                   (coll? x) (every? machine-data? x)\n   934\t                   :else false)\n   935\t             (machine-data? (meta x))))))\n   905\t                                  (deep! d :unsupported-value)\n   906\t                                  (let [items (mapv #(value-bytes % (inc d)) x)]\n   907\t                                    (concat-buffers (cons (write-head 4 (count items)) items)))))\n   908\t    (set? x) (meta-bytes x d #(set-bytes x %))\n   909\t    (or (list? x) (seq? x)) (meta-bytes x d #(list-bytes x %))\n   910\t    :else (refuse :unsupported-value (type-name x))))\n   911\t\n   912\t\n   913\t(defn encode\n   914\t  \"The canonical CBOR payload bytes of one supported value (byte[] on the\n   915\t   JVM, Uint8Array on JavaScript, Uint8List on Dart). Refuses unsupported\n   916\t   values, unpaired surrogates, nesting deeper than max-depth, and\n   917\t   collections whose members collide after normalization.\"\n   918\t  [value]\n   919\t  (value-bytes value 1))\n   920\t\n   921\t\n   922\t(defn copy-bytes\n   923\t  \"A fresh copy of host byte array bs, so a holder's snapshot cannot be\n   924\t   changed through the array a caller keeps.\"\n   925\t  [bs]\n   926\t  (bytes/slice bs 0 (bytes/length bs)))\n   927\t\n   928\t\n   929\t(defn encoded-compare\n   930\t  \"Order two supported values by their canonical encodings: the shorter\n   931\t   encoding first, then unsigned bytewise (note: canonical map and set encoding\n   932\t   uses pure unsigned bytewise lexicographical order per RFC 8949, not length-first).\n   933\t   Returns -1, 0 or 1. It encodes both values, so callers reserve it for the\n   934\t   rare case that needs it (the min/max tie-break after numeric order has already tied).\"\n   935\t  [a b]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"agent_message","text":"DO NOT LAND\n\n1. **P1-a remains open.** The address-membership check is gone, and directly passed or prelude-constructed packets retain per-occurrence origins. But packets are ordinary vectors and the macro environment exposes collection primitives such as `first`, `rest`, and `conj` ([vm.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:398)). A macro can rebuild an identical packet container from an operand without constructing its syntax. That loses the container metadata, so the fallback classifies every application as constructed by the new expansion ([macro.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1232)). The retained syntax consequently receives the wrong origin.\n\n2. **U2:** The publisher uses an explicit admission, then a composition-owned scope token, then the deterministic specification default ([publish.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:277)). The default makes identical undeclared republication one event, as stated in the request. I found no interaction with this round’s expansion changes.\n\n3. **Metadata isolation fails at content addressing.** A macro can pass an operand packet as the value to `yin/literal` ([macro.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:667)). The constructor hashes that value before output validation ([macro.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:505)); the packet now carries a function in its metadata, and the CBOR encoder processes collection metadata ([cbor.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:908)). This can turn a previously plain packet literal into a body error. In the normal constructor path, final packets are rebuilt from rows, so the tracking metadata itself does not enter the final tree or store.\n\n4. **Site table:** The row shape remains `[origin root-address path :yang/call-site 1]` ([macro.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1387)). Origins flow from the recorded correspondence through the occurrence walk, but the metadata-loss case in point 1 causes the record—and therefore the table—to report an incorrect origin.\n\n5. **New defects:** The packet-container reconstruction and packet-as-literal cases above are the blockers introduced by the metadata approach.\n\nThe ruling file was absent at the supplied path in this checkout; this assessment uses the U1–U3 semantics stated in the request. Review was static and read-only; no tests were run."}}
